WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.570
So I created a training platform, and this project is going to explore

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.130
practical training platform problem.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:11.670
What happens when the worker fails halfway through a run?

00:00:12.025 --> 00:00:16.965
So what I built was a durable solution

00:00:16.965 --> 00:00:21.845
that validates a specific configuration, trains

00:00:21.845 --> 00:00:23.985
a model, and saves its checkpoints.

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:29.493
And after saving its checkpoints, if it fails, deliberately kills

00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:34.413
the worker and verifies that it recovered from that checkpoint.

00:00:35.393 --> 00:00:39.633
Now the dataset that I used for this

00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.233
is BANKING77 dataset from Hugging Face.

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:48.693
It has about 77 intent categories

00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:52.593
for a ton of banking questions.

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:57.764
The reason I used this dataset was because I wanted to

00:00:57.764 --> 00:01:02.236
simulate customer service, question

00:01:02.236 --> 00:01:07.200
-based routing via intent, for either an internal ticketing,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.260
queuing system,

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.280
or for answering questions based off

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.480
of user intent.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.420
So what I did is I pinned the dataset revision and

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.000
verified its content hash, giving it a content hash key.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.360
Then I prepared splits between 2,464

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.880
training examples and 154 validation

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.720
examples.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.300
Now before allocating a worker,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:44.280
the platform checks the configuration, dataset hash, and the batch settings.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.480
The CPU recording uses a tiny classifier to exercise the infrastructure,

00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:54.873
and the separate GPU extension fine-tunes a pretrained language

00:01:54.873 --> 00:01:56.393
model with LoRA.

00:01:57.453 --> 00:02:02.313
This configuration that I have set up completes 20

00:02:02.313 --> 00:02:05.352
optimizer updates on a local CPU,

00:02:06.132 --> 00:02:09.053
and then saves a checkpoint on every 5 updates.

00:02:09.515 --> 00:02:12.195
So runtime and retries are bounded.

00:02:13.262 --> 00:02:17.632
The recording contains actual command output, so these are the outputs

00:02:17.632 --> 00:02:20.732
that I was doing when it was running locally. This baseline gives a

00:02:20.732 --> 00:02:23.612
recovery run concrete reference for comparison.

00:02:24.652 --> 00:02:29.392
Next, I injected a real worker failure at update 7

00:02:29.392 --> 00:02:31.352
using SIGKILL.

00:02:31.352 --> 00:02:35.086
The run correctly reports that failure.

00:02:35.586 --> 00:02:40.246
And it jumps back to its most recent complete checkpoint,

00:02:41.086 --> 00:02:42.186
which was update 5.

00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:45.694
Recovery restores more than just the model.

00:02:45.894 --> 00:02:49.854
It restores the optimizer, the learning rate scheduler, and the random

00:02:49.854 --> 00:02:53.114
number generator state, and its position in the training data.

00:02:53.754 --> 00:02:57.874
So it doesn't skip over any of the datasets.

00:02:59.127 --> 00:03:01.087
Now, this worker resumes from 5,

00:03:01.827 --> 00:03:02.327
stage 5,

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:08.047
recomputes update 6 and 7, and then continues on through

00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:08.587
its batches.

00:03:09.247 --> 00:03:14.207
The raw log preserves repeated updates while canonical view selects

00:03:14.207 --> 00:03:15.987
the latest attempt for each update.

00:03:17.047 --> 00:03:21.391
Now, after finishing successfully, I wanted to also establish that

00:03:22.711 --> 00:03:25.291
it recovered correctly.

00:03:25.711 --> 00:03:30.671
and what we do is compare 7 state fields against

00:03:30.671 --> 00:03:35.291
an uninterrupted baseline using relative zero tolerance,

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:40.511
and an absolute tolerance of 1 times 10 to the negative 7.

00:03:41.141 --> 00:03:44.881
And when we do that, the comparison passes.

00:03:46.361 --> 00:03:48.801
So you can also see the recovery history directly.

00:03:49.581 --> 00:03:50.641
Restoration at 5,

00:03:51.341 --> 00:03:51.901
completion at 20,

00:03:52.661 --> 00:03:55.921
22 raw metric rows, and 20 canonical rows.

00:03:56.991 --> 00:04:00.751
Now, for evaluation, we use the same holdout system.

00:04:01.551 --> 00:04:05.211
We take the paired predictions and the per-class metrics,

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:10.751
and we take what makes model iteration traceable to be a specific dataset

00:04:10.751 --> 00:04:13.871
configuration and checkpoint.

00:04:14.182 --> 00:04:17.502
Locally, I also tested two Ray workers using PyTorch,

00:04:18.202 --> 00:04:19.462
distributed training with Gloo,

00:04:20.462 --> 00:04:22.902
including recovery after a worker failure.

00:04:23.313 --> 00:04:24.793
Those are separate measured runs.

00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:29.373
Since this was a smaller scale, distributed execution was slower because

00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:33.353
setup and coordination outweighed the useful computation.

00:04:34.383 --> 00:04:35.743
And so inside of Databricks,

00:04:36.723 --> 00:04:39.283
we verify the dataset preparation,

00:04:40.683 --> 00:04:44.627
delta table, their writes and their reads, an artifact export, and some

00:04:44.627 --> 00:04:45.587
result imports.

00:04:46.732 --> 00:04:51.012
Each of the eight run bundles was imported twice,

00:04:51.772 --> 00:04:55.872
and the resulting tables contain zero duplicate key groups.

00:04:56.172 --> 00:04:58.552
Raw metrics retain retry history,

00:04:59.412 --> 00:05:04.412
and the canonical view supports comparison without double counting

00:05:04.412 --> 00:05:06.252
some of these repeated updates.

00:05:06.708 --> 00:05:10.683
These results came

00:05:10.683 --> 00:05:13.943
from an executed Databricks notebook.

00:05:14.923 --> 00:05:19.063
This is a separate run on a Kaggle Tesla T4 GPU.

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:24.543
It uses the pretrained SmolLM2 model with about

00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:26.743
135 million parameters.

00:05:27.363 --> 00:05:30.623
LoRA updates only 460,800

00:05:30.623 --> 00:05:35.583
adapter parameters while

00:05:35.583 --> 00:05:36.803
keeping the base model frozen.

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:41.263
And I completed the two update smoke tests.

00:05:41.762 --> 00:05:46.342
And then at update three, I forced a real failure, restored

00:05:46.342 --> 00:05:47.162
checkpoint two,

00:05:47.962 --> 00:05:49.082
continue to update four.

00:05:50.142 --> 00:05:54.002
The recovery checkpoint matched the uninterrupted reference across the

00:05:54.002 --> 00:05:56.602
complete saved state with zero tensor difference.

00:05:57.662 --> 00:06:00.562
The recovery correctness and model quality have separate checks.

00:06:00.562 --> 00:06:05.362
After the single GPU recovery test, I compared that base model and adapter

00:06:05.362 --> 00:06:06.962
on the complete heldout set.

00:06:08.042 --> 00:06:13.022
The main GPU experiment completed 100 updates and its first attempt

00:06:13.022 --> 00:06:15.502
hit a 45 minute deadline during the evaluation.

00:06:16.382 --> 00:06:18.882
So I preserved that failure and restored the final checkpoint.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:23.550
After batching generation, I resumed the evaluation completing all

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.850
3,076 tests without repeating training.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:33.710
There was a macro F1 increase from zero to about

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:38.318
0.049, as 96 predictions

00:06:38.318 --> 00:06:38.928
improved.

00:06:40.358 --> 00:06:44.818
But more than 95% of adapter responses were still invalid labels at

00:06:44.818 --> 00:06:45.258
that point.

00:06:45.617 --> 00:06:50.097
The evaluation at that point exposed the next engineering problem, which

00:06:50.097 --> 00:06:51.717
was label generation.

00:06:52.587 --> 00:06:55.117
Prompt did not list all 77 choices,

00:06:56.417 --> 00:06:58.857
and the base produced no exact valid labels.

00:06:59.467 --> 00:07:02.927
So what I did was I downloaded the reports and checkpoints, checking

00:07:02.927 --> 00:07:03.567
their hashes,

00:07:04.347 --> 00:07:06.147
and stopped the GPU session.

00:07:06.577 --> 00:07:11.343
And then I imported all of them into Databricks, all four of

00:07:11.343 --> 00:07:11.983
LoRA runs.

00:07:12.524 --> 00:07:17.484
And notice that the counts stayed at 111 raw metrics

00:07:17.484 --> 00:07:22.124
and 3,076 paired predictions

00:07:22.124 --> 00:07:25.444
with 110 canonical metrics and zero duplicates.

00:07:26.504 --> 00:07:30.044
So the architecture separates local GPU compute

00:07:31.102 --> 00:07:35.762
from our Databricks reporting and transferred every

00:07:35.762 --> 00:07:37.402
verified object between them.

00:07:37.702 --> 00:07:42.642
If you look at the linked FigJam board, you'll see the documents

00:07:42.642 --> 00:07:43.762
on our system,

00:07:44.502 --> 00:07:48.782
our architectural decision records, the training recovery and the import

00:07:48.782 --> 00:07:49.202
flows,

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:51.502
and the system design behind it.

00:07:51.802 --> 00:07:54.662
The deployment stage adds durable shared storage,

00:07:55.582 --> 00:07:56.762
managed job orchestration,

00:07:57.533 --> 00:07:58.793
operational monitoring,

00:08:00.003 --> 00:08:04.963
following the multi-node load and the host loss recovery validation.

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:08.179
This result is something reproducible,

00:08:09.079 --> 00:08:10.399
training platform.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:12.239
With a test of failure recovery,

00:08:13.079 --> 00:08:17.519
GPU fine-tuning and traceable evaluation using standardized libraries.
